# CHAPTER 4 · SEARCH, SUBSTITUTE, GLOBAL

Bulk edits are where vim humiliates other editors. Three layers:
refined search, `:s`{vim} with ranges and captures, and `:g`{vim} to
run ANY command on every matching line.

ATTENTION: exercises here say `:s`{vim} (current line) on purpose.
A stray `:%s`{vim} will also rewrite the lesson text around the
exercises — harmless (reopen with :Tutor), but you were warned.

## 4.1 SEARCH, REFINED

`/\vpattern`{normal} — "very magic": +, (, ), | work like in normal
regexes, no backslash forest. Use `\v`{normal} by default.
`*`{normal} / `#`{normal} search the exact word under the cursor.
`/pattern/e`{normal} lands at the END of the match.
`:set ignorecase smartcase`{vim} — lowercase searches match any case,
capitals search exactly (already worth putting in your options.lua).

## 4.2 THE KILLER COMBO: * THEN cgn THEN .

`gn`{normal} is a text object meaning "the next search match".
`cgn`{normal} = change the next match. And it repeats with
`.`{normal}. So: `*`{normal} on a word, `cgn`{normal}, type the
replacement, `<Esc>`{normal}, then press `.`{normal} on each further
hit — or `n`{normal} to SKIP one. It is :%s with a human in the loop.

EXERCISE 1: The three lines below share one placeholder identifier.
Put the cursor on it, press `*`{normal}, then `cgn`{normal} and type
the name baz_qux,
`<Esc>`{normal}, then `.`{normal} `.`{normal} (it wraps around to the
remaining hits):

count = foo_bar + 1
print(foo_bar)
return foo_bar + offset

## 4.3 SUBSTITUTE: RANGES, FLAGS, CAPTURES

`:[range]s/pat/rep/flags`{vim}. Ranges: `%`{normal} whole file,
`.,+4`{normal} this line plus four, `'<,'>`{normal} the visual
selection, `'a,'b`{normal} between marks. Flags: g all matches in the
line, c confirm each, i ignore case. `&`{normal} repeats the last
substitute on the current line.

EXERCISE 2: On the line below run `:s/\vthe/THE/g`{vim} — the g flag
hits all three:

the quick fox and the lazy dog and the cat

Capture groups: \v makes ( ) capture directly; \1 \2 refer back.

EXERCISE 3: Swap the name order on the line below with
`:s/\v(\w+), (\w+)/\2 \1/`{vim}:

Doe, Jane

`\zs`{normal} and `\ze`{normal} trim the match: the pattern must
appear, but only the part between them is replaced.

EXERCISE 4: Bump only the major version below with
`:s/version-\zs\d/9/`{vim} (matches the digit only if "version-"
precedes it — the prefix itself is untouched):

version-1.2.3

## 4.4 GLOBAL: RUN A COMMAND ON EVERY MATCHING LINE

`:g/pattern/cmd`{vim} runs cmd on every matching line, `:v`{vim} on
every NON-matching line. Classics:

    :g/DEBUG/d        delete every line containing DEBUG
    :v/error/d        keep only lines containing error
    :g/^def /t$       copy every function header to the end of file
    :g/TODO/m0        move every TODO line to the top

EXERCISE 5: Strip the debug markers from the two lines below with one
command: `:g/ -- DEBUG$/s/ -- DEBUG$//`{vim} (the $ anchors to line
end, so nothing else in this file matches):

data_out <= shift_reg(7); -- DEBUG
irq_flag <= '1'; -- DEBUG

`:g`{vim} also combines with `normal`{vim} — run keystrokes on every
matching line — but that story belongs to the macros chapter.

## WORKOUT

In one of your real VHDL or Python files (read-only experiments,
just undo after): rename a signal with `*`{normal}+`cgn`{normal}+
`.`{normal} skipping one false positive; delete every commented-out
line in a block with `:g`{vim}; swap two arguments with a capture
group.

Key reading: [gn](gn), [:global](:global), [/\zs](/\zs),
[s/\1](s/\1)

